# GitHub Actions lifts the `run:` blocks straight out of these YAML files and pipes them to
# bash. A CRLF committed into a workflow arrives as a literal \r inside the script, and bash
# fails with `$'\r': command not found` - a red job that has nothing to do with the code.
# Checking these out as LF makes that failure impossible whatever core.autocrlf a
# contributor happens to have set, on Windows or otherwise.
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the Python sources: LF everywhere keeps diffs and container COPY steps
# predictable, and avoids a "\r" turning up in a shebang line.
*.py   text eol=lf
